  "live_records": {
    "q-38909": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38909",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?",
      "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
