    "q-340192": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340192",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/6d3cbdc4a0158ebc827c2f991b446f0b9464322f8596b97176af55aa39b4a72d.png)\n\nBased on the graph of $y=f(x)$  shown above, the function $f$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at $x=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes.",
