    "q-104710": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104710",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/0cfcb2f5e07b52b28cb40ed293996fa3699aab4759093513f7386a4d8c3024c1.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?",
      "worked_solution": "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-104710-a",
