    "q-48242": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48242",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-4)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-4)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-1$ and $x=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
