    "q-340208": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340208",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x}$ \nhas discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptote, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(3x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptote by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3},0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)$ has discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
