      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x}$ has infinite discontinuities.",
      "worked_solution": "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{x(3x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
