    "q-340229": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340229",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)(x-4)}{(x-4)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)\\cancel{(x-4)}}{\\cancel{(x-4)}(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-5}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+4=0\\,⟹\\,x=-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
