    "q-340259": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340259",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
