    "q-104854": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104854",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
